w I  came to be here and there was an abiding  purpose I have enthusiasms you know I  like motorcycles I like dogs I like you  know and and some of those I've stuck to  for a long time but other enthusiasms  come and go  whereas larger purposes you can't  achieve them if you don't commit to them  for a very long time and that's reason  talking my wife and I have children you  don't understand it till you do it but  you know our oldest child is 32 now and  she's still our child and we have to be  there for her and those are long-term  purposes right reason speaks to those  passions tend to become and go right and  if it takes at least six or eight years  fully to change the government passions  don't tend to last that long  convictions do reason does and you're  arguing about it all the time all along  the way and that means that Madison says  that these arrangements here this  connection between the people and the  government it gives power to the  government locates ultimate authority in  the people prescribes how the people  must must hold opinions over time to  make them effective this is a set of  arrangements what does this make it  possible for them to do they can  delegate the authority  to more than one place you can give the  legislative power to the Congress in  article 1 of the Constitution you can  give the executive power to the  president in article 2 of the  Constitution they each have their own  relationship with the sovereign people  they are quite equal as regards  Authority you can give all the judicial  power in article 3 to the courts and you  can use article 1 and article 2  institutions to combine to appoint the  judges you can leave most of the power  in the hands of the state and you can  protect that in article 4 of the  Constitution you see the symmetry and  the strength of that all made possible  by this consent and representation is  the sovereign was in the government  whatever part of the government in  Madison rights is over and over 